 "name": "Campus Courtyard Healing Effect (semantic differential and PRS)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Cao & Huang (2023), Research on the Healing Effect Evaluation of Campus' Small-Scale Courtyard Based on the Method of Semantic Differential and the Perceived Restorative Scale, Sustainability 15(10): 8369. Each participant is randomly assigned one of three small courtyards between dormitory buildings on a high-density university campus in Beijing (I: large lawn and a badminton court; II: regular rows of trees and shrubs along a straight path; III: irregular planting of pines and shrubs along a winding path). They look at a photograph of it, give age, gender and field of study, then rate it on 17 seven-point semantic-differential word pairs in three groups (ornamental, uniqueness, activity; e.g. barren-lush, chaos-harmonious, oppressive-relaxed, dim-bright) and on the 18 items of the Chinese Perceived Restorativeness Scale (being away, extent, fascination, compatibility; 1-7). Answers are keyed by courtyard. In the original, 165 students gave valid answers and both scales ranked Courtyard III > II > I. Photographs are from the paper's Figure 3 (CC BY 4.0), used unmodified with credit to the authors; the template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper showed three photographs of each courtyard but printed only one, so one photograph per courtyard is shown; (2) the courtyard is assigned at random, the original distributed separate questionnaires per courtyard; (3) English wording, the original was in Chinese; the PRS items use the paper's own English translation; (4) the PRS anchors (1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree) are not printed in the paper and are assumed; (5) the photograph stays visible at the top while answering.",
